# Call-stack diffs with calldiff

When writing a plan, design doc, PR description, or end-of-task change summary, include a call-stack diff so reviewers can see how call flow changed.

## When to use

Run calldiff when the change rewires call flow: new, removed, or moved callees; refactored control flow; extracted or inlined functions.

Skip for pure UI/copy, styling, config, dependency, translation, and docs-only changes.

## How to run

Use `npx calldiff@latest`. Defaults compare HEAD vs the working tree.

```bash
npx calldiff@latest diff --file apps/desktop/src/pages/settings.tsx
npx calldiff@latest diff main --entry install_mod
```

Other useful commands:

```bash
npx calldiff@latest tree --entry install_mod
npx calldiff@latest reach --entry install_mod --to persist_vpk
```

Scope with `--file` / `--entry` and `--maxDepth`. Keep the pasted tree to the entrypoints that changed.

Paste the output in a fenced `diff` block.

## Caveats

- Calldiff is syntactic (tree-sitter). Dynamic calls and trait-object dispatch will not resolve.
- Grammars download on first use into `~/.cache/calldiff/grammars`.
- If calldiff produces nothing useful, say so in one line and fall back to a hand-written call tree per the [show-me](../../.agents/skills/show-me/SKILL.md) skill rather than omitting the flow.
